Date: Tue, 9 Mar 93 15:02:16 -0800
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: ECON: Winners and Losers in Technology

Robin Hanson writes:

>> I'm sure Tim May could tell us about chip or memory technology that
>> looked better on some fundamental physical grounds, but just couldn't
>> catch up with the larger investment made in the standard technology.

Yes. Many, many examples of technologies which never really made it.
Charge-coupled devices (CCDs), silicon-on-sapphire (even used in the title
of a Marc Stiegler co-authored novel, "Valentina: Soul on Sapphire"), DMOS,
e-beam addresses memories, Josephson Junctions, holographic memories,
Integrated Injection Logic ("I-squared L"), HEMTs, and an and on.

Our own Eric Raymond, in "The Hacker's Dictionary," calls ideas like these
(though not these in particular, of course) "vannevars" :

>Permit me, if you will, the luxury of quoting myself:
>
>:vannevar: /van'*-var/ n. A bogus technological prediction or a
>   foredoomed engineering concept, esp: one that fails by implicitly
>   assuming that technologies develop linearly, incrementally, and in
>   isolation from one another when in fact the learning curve tends to
>   be highly nonlinear, revolutions are common, and competition is the
>   rule.  The prototype was Vannevar Bush's prediction of
>   `electronic brains' the size of the Empire State Building with a
>   Niagara-Falls-equivalent cooling system for their tubes and relays,
>   made at a time when the semiconductor effect had already been
>   demonstrated.  Other famous vannevars have included magnetic-bubble
>   memory, LISP machines, videotex, and a paper from the
>   late~1970s that computed a purported ultimate limit on areal
>   density for ICs that was in fact less than the routine densities of
>   5 years later.

I favor Robin's slant on this over Eric's. The ideas were not necessarily
bogus (though a few were), but other technologies moved in and occupied the
niche and thereby denied the losers any chance to recoup their costs and
thrive. 

LISP machines are a good example--was the concept of a powerful, symbolic
processing oriented workstation a bad idea, a bogus prediction? No, and in
fact many Symbolics machines are still in use where a powerful, integrated
environment for complex software development is needed.

Had the simultaneous proliferation of Sun-type workstations and the sudden
popularity of C and C++ not happened, Symbolics might now occupy the niche
that Sun and Apollo (now part of H-P) moved into. (I won't get into a
religious debate about the merits of LISP vs. C, or incremental
compilation, objects, etc.)

It's always very easy in retrospect to declare a failed product to be a 
"bogus" idea. As someone who lost money in several failed or moribund
companies (Symbolics, Zycad, etc.), I can tell you the ideas did not _then_
look bogus. And the companies I made the most money on, Intel, Apple, and
Sun, were the ones with the most straightforward extropolations of
technology.

In fact, had Apple failed with the Mac--which came real close to happening,
circa 1985, and which may yet happen (what with the huge popularity of
Windows)--then some would be chortling over the "vannevar" that Apple
pulled. Likewise, prior to Windows 3.0 it sure did look like Microsoft had
pulled a vannevar with its feeble attempts to get into the Mac-like OS
market.

And it can plausibly be argued that Sun's success came specifically from
anticipating a _linear extrapolotion of trends_, rather than from adopting
the more nonlinear "revolutions" like AI and symbolic processing
(Symbolics, LMI), very long instruction word computers (Multiflow,
Cydrome), neural nets, and so on. The Sun workstation was just a simple
processor computer running Berkeley Unix. More power to them--they guessed
right.

Speaking of "vannevars," is hypertext considered to be a vannevar? (And
ironically, some friends of mine just got a multimillion dollar financing
round for their software company, called "Memex"...the name of Vannevar
Bush's scheme for hypertext.)

Eric's dictionary is a lot of fun. My discovery of the alpha particle
effect is even there, under "bit rot" or "cosmic rays," or somesuch, left
over from the Guy Steele edition I guess, so I can't complain. But some of
the jokes are obviously cheap shots, directed at the losers.

Why is history written by the winners? A famous quote explains it all:

"Why doth treason never prosper? For if treason doth prosper, none dare
call it treason."

-Tim May


