Date: Tue, 9 Mar 93 17:39:46 CST
From: eder@hsvaic.boeing.com (Dani Eder)
Subject: D-FENS: Defending Ourselves

To correct Mr. Swank's comment on 'Ancient Castles':

(note: I'm a student of castles)

Correctly, what we commonly think of as "castles" mainly existed
in the medieval period, although walled cities go back to ancient
times (defined as before the fall of Rome).  In fact, for most of
history, cities were defended by walls.

As far as escape tunnels, the castles I am aware of mostly did
not have 'escape tunnels'.  They relied on strategic location
and solidity of the walls to deter capture.  A good example is
Harlech castle in Wales, built by Edward I of England (he was
subjugating Wales at the time).  It was built on a stone
promontory on the Welsh coast about 100 ft high.  A culvert
was dug in the stone about 20 ft deep to isolate the castle
from the rest of the land-side hill on which it was built.

On the seaward side a stairway winds down the rock face to
sea level, where a boat could dock with supplies.  

The culvert was traversed by a drawbridge.  When raised, the
castle was defended by three dozen men against the Welsh forces.

The other principal defense besides hilltops was to dig a
moat, or create one by damming a stream.  In this case, it was
kept foul, by dropping wastes into it, and also served as a
physical barrier as well as a nauseous one.

Forests were kept away from castle sites, as they blocked
the defenders view of approaching enemies, and nearby trees
afforded an easy source of climbing poles (fell a tree within
it's own height of the wall and you have a ramp.  Another
reason for clear space around the castle was the wood consumed
for construction (scaffolding, floors) and living (fireplaces).

A tunnel would have to be cut through rock by hand at most sites,
and would be underwater at the moated sites.  The most frequent
occurrence of tunnels was actually in the process of beseiging
a castle.  The 'sappers' would dig under the wall, support it
with timbers, then fire the timbers in an attempt to make the
wall collapse (the removal of the supports and the de-hydration
of the cement contributed to this).  A collpsed wall then provided
a breach for the invaders to enter through.

