	Yes, I have read the book, and I have even promised to write a
review of the book for EXPONENT.

	I'll save most of my comments for the essay, but I will mention
a few things here.  The book is excellent.  The subtitle of the book is:
"Restoring the Character Ethic".  The 'self-help' material written in
the 19th century was very different from the usual self-help material
of the 20th century (such as _How to Win Friends and Influence People_).
The 19th century self-help material promoted a character ethic.  People
were told to find success by improving their habits, which involves
changing their character.  The 20th century self-help material left this 
basic approach by focusing on people-skills and positive thinking.
There is nothing wrong with those things, but the author's point is that
they won't be very effective until you change your _character_ first.

